import crate::emit::pattern_text::expanded::expanded
import redox_ast::Pattern

-- | One round of synonym substitution, recursively.
export(crate) module expanded
-- | A pattern with its children expanded and itself left alone.
export(crate) module rebuilt
-- | A synonym body with a formal parameter replaced by an actual argument.
export(crate) module substituted

-- | A pattern's Rust text, with plan 87's synonyms expanded.
-- |
-- | ```text
-- | pattern Origin = Point { x = 0, y = 0 }
-- | pattern Both a b = (Some a, Some b)
-- |
-- | Origin      ->  Point { x: 0, y: 0 }
-- | Both l r    ->  (Some(l), Some(r))
-- | ```
-- |
-- | **The substitution is textual and happens here, at render time.** That is
-- | plan 56 §11.3's placement rule: `redox_fmt::format` reads the same tree and
-- | must keep printing the author's `Origin` as `Origin`, so expansion may not
-- | be visible before `emit_with_map` installs
-- | [`SynonymScope`](crate::emit::synonym_scope::SynonymScope).
-- |
-- | Every emitter that renders a pattern goes through this rather than calling
-- | `Pattern::rust_text` directly, which is what makes the expansion reach all
-- | the pattern positions — an arm, a bind, an `if … is`, a `while … is`, a
-- | control-flow binder and a clause's binder row. Plan 87's precondition 2
-- | expected six *entry points in the parser* and found one; the six are on
-- | this side.
-- |
-- | # Why rustc loses nothing
-- |
-- | The emitted pattern is the one the author would have written out, so rustc
-- | performs every check it would have performed on that form —
-- | exhaustiveness, field names, types, binding modes. Doc 03 §2's corollary
-- | holds in both directions: Redox checks nothing new, and rustc checks
-- | nothing less.
export(crate) pattern_text : &Pattern -> String
pattern_text pattern = .rust_text (expanded pattern 0)
